<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      IsPackable stays INLINE here, never hoisted into shared props. The API-baseline coverage gate finds
      shipped projects by TEXT-MATCHING this element in the csproj, so hoisting it would make this package
      invisible to the gate: it would ship with no baseline and nothing would say so. The mobile packages
      already documented this trap; one duplicated line is the cheaper mistake.
    -->
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <PackageId>Shenora.Media</PackageId>
    <Description>Shenora media: the TRANSLATION LAYER that makes a file a webview cannot decode playable, and never more than that. A pipeline — PROBE a Matroska header with no external tool, PLAN the minimum move per stream (direct / remux / transcode / unsupported), SERVE the result as a converted file or an HLS segment stream over the shell's interceptor, and TRANSFORM it — a managed Matroska-to-MP4 remuxer that copies every frame untouched, plus a seam for anything needing a real encoder. Ships NO codec list and vendors NO engine binary: the mechanism is the kit's, the codec policy is the application's.</Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    net10.0 — deliberately NOT a platform TFM, and it is enforced rather than asserted (D41). App logic
    names `Shenora.Media` and never `Shenora.Media.{Platform}`, which is what makes one media call site
    work on three shells; `samples/Shenora.Sample.Logic` is a net10.0 project that turns RED if a platform
    type reaches app logic, so this package has to compile there. It inherits net10.0 from
    src/Directory.Build.props — restated nowhere, because a second declaration is a second thing to drift.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      Shenora.Core, and the edge is REAL rather than declared — it is the SERVE stage that draws it, and
      nothing else. `Probe/` and `Plan/` are pure functions over their own data and would compile against
      no reference at all; `Serve/` registers middleware on `IWebViewInterceptor`, answers with
      `WebViewResourceRequest`/`WebViewResourceResponse`, runs the slow work through `IMissionScheduler` +
      `PathClaims`/`MissionKey`, publishes its output atomically through `Files.BeginReplace`, keys the
      cache with `DerivedCacheKey`, and logs through `AppCallback`. All of those describe a host↔page
      resource exchange or portable machinery, which is why they live in Core (D2a) — this package adds the
      media DECISIONS on top and owns none of that plumbing.

      ⚠ The direction is one-way and must stay so: Core never references this package, so an app that
      never touches media pays for none of it (D40).
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Shenora.Core\Shenora.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
